00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.510
This isn't really the United States.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:18.210
This map—while it may display the technical,
internationally recognized borders of the

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:22.310
country—just is not fully accurate.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.670
It should look more like this.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:30.099
That’s because the 89 thousand square miles
or 230 thousand square kilometers now missing

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:36.300
from this map are filled by 326 Indian reservations,
and the Native American Tribes that manage

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.410
these lands are recognized by the US Federal
Government as sovereigns.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:47.461
That’s to say, while these may not be countries,
they are, by most measures, independent, sovereign

00:00:47.461 --> 00:00:49.050
nations.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:54.250
Collectively, reservations account for 2.5%
of the US’ total landmass, and together

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:59.470
would represent the 12th physically largest
state—between Michigan and Minnesota.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.460
Truly the only commonality between each of
the reservations is the system through which

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.280
they are recognized by the US Federal Government.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.510
They range dramatically is size, system, and
function.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.420
The smallest, for example, is the Likely Rancheria
in Northern California.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:20.350
At just 1.32 acres, it’s physically similar
in size to many suburban home plots, and is

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.700
used as the cemetery of the Pit River Tribe.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.630
There are also plenty of larger Reservations
without any permanent population.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.800
For example, the Ontonagon Indian Reservation
in Michigans’ Upper Peninsula is mainly

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.220
used by a branch of the Lake Superior Chippewa
Tribe as hunting and fishing land and the

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.420
small Snoqualmie Reservation is almost entirely
composed of the tribe’s casino.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:46.670
But of course, a majority of reservations
are inhabited by anywhere between one and

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.729
174,000 people.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:55.590
At the upper end of that spectrum, there’s
the physically largest, most populous, and—by

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:01.310
many measures—most politically developed
Indian Reservation—the Navajo Nation.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:06.130
Centered around northeastern Arizona, but
passing into Utah and New Mexico as well,

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.979
the Navajo nation is physically larger than
ten US states, and has a population greater

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.040
than that of 18 UN-recognized countries.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:21.310
Now, according to the US, the Navajo Nation
is sovereign, but it’s certainly not a country,

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.380
so what is it?

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:30.480
Now, like all Indian Reservations, the story
of how the Navajo Nation came to be is complex

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.480
and tragic.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:36.280
Its Diné people have inhabited the Southwest
since their migration from Arctic America

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.760
around the 15th century AD, but the path that
directly led to the establishment of the reservation

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.820
began with the Long Walk.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.950
After years of conflict between European Americans
and the Diné, the US Federal Government directed

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.480
the Army to end what was, in their eyes, the
“Navajo problem.”

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.010
Soldiers descended on Navajo land, destroyed
much of their civilization, and one by one

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:04.830
forced bands of Diné to walk 400 miles or
650 kilometers from their homelands to Fort

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:10.480
Sumner, in New Mexico, where they were imprisoned
on cramped, unproductive land.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.290
Hundreds died during the walk, and thousands
more while imprisoned.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.610
After a few years, though, the government
recognized that they had made a mistake—the

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:24.200
camp was costly to run due to its low agricultural
yields, and they realized that the land from

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.040
which the Diné had come was not what the
US would consider “useful” for its settlers.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.790
In exchange for a number of provisions aimed
at assimilation, the Navajo and the US signed

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.250
the Treaty of Bosque Redondo—ending the
internment, and effectively establishing the

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.840
Navajo’s reservation around their homelands.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:46.450
Still, though, in the decades that followed,
the reservation looked little like the nation

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:47.560
of today.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:53.420
Change accelerated in 1921, though, when the
Aztec Oil Syndicate struck oil one mile south

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.800
of the town of Aztec, New Mexico.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.750
This would go on to be developed into the
first commercial well in the region—attracting

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.450
considerable attention nationwide.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:07.909
While this first site was outside the reservation,
large oil companies soon recognized that there

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:11.260
were almost certainly productive sites within
Navajo borders.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:15.580
US Secretary of the Interior Albert Bacon
Fall, who was quite friendly with the oil

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.160
and gas industry—and later would become
the first presidential cabinet member in history

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.520
to go to jail after accepting bribes from
the industry—recognized that further oil

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.690
and gas development in the region would require
the Navajo’s system of governance to look

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.340
a little more like the US’.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.440
At the time, the reservation was administered
through a decentralized, somewhat informal,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.229
traditional form of governance which made
negotiations with big American companies difficult.

00:04:41.229 --> 00:04:46.330
Therefore, Fall organized a Navajo business
council, with three positions filled by three

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:53.370
of his appointees—all of whom were Navajo,
but also friends of Fall or the industry.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.520
However, Bureau of Indian Affairs Commissioner
Charles H Burke recognized that this was far

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.120
from a democratic process—an unelected council
making decisions on behalf of all the Diné

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.240
people—so he worked to set up an elected
tribal council—the new core of the Nation’s

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.880
legislative system.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.740
Still today, that council serves the same
purpose, but its establishment snowballed

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:20.030
the Navajo government into something far more
formal, sprawling, and western.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:25.820
The sovereignty of the Navajo Nation is best
examined through its capital—Window Rock.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:31.110
Its streets are lined with offices of all
the agencies and organizations running the

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:35.750
government functions that the Navajo Nation
handles itself: the Division of Public Safety,

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:40.010
the Window Rock Police Department, the Office
of Community Development, the Navajo Nation

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:44.220
Veterans Administration, the Navajo Housing
Authority, the Navajo Nation Telecommunications

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.900
and Utility Agency, the Fish and Wildlife
Department, the Heritage and Historic Preservation

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.960
Office, the Tribal Courts, the Supreme Court,
the Office of Vital Records, the Council Chambers,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.900
and the Navajo Nation Office of the President
and Vice President.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.919
Many similarly-named structures could also
be found in county, state, and national capitals

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:06.640
across the US and world, but there are some
missing.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.420
Also dotting the landscape of Window Rock
are features like a branch of the United States

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:16.000
Postal Service; an office of the Arizona Motor
Vehicle Department; Arizona State Route 264,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.090
maintained by the Arizona Department of Transportation;
and an airport administered and regulated

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.199
by the American FAA.

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:27.120
Adding to that, one sees many of the chain
services and stores of the United States—Aflac,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:34.230
H&amp;R Block, Ace Hardware, Wells Fargo, Chevron,
Bank of America, Quality Inn, McDonald’s,

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:35.230
and more.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:39.460
However, there is also a small, unique business
landscape.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.800
There are Navajo Petroleum gas stations, owned
by the Navajo oil and gas company of the same

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.300
name; Bashas’ Diné Market which, while
owned and operated by the region-wide Bashas’

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.250
brand, is a unique sub-brand carrying goods
especially in-demand by Diné like Blue Bird

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:57.090
flour and mutton; and there’s the Navajo
Times which, while originally ran by the government,

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:00.190
is now a for-profit, financially independent
newspaper.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:05.940
So, the government and economy of the Navajo
Nation is an amalgamation of Navajo and American

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:13.319
features so, considering that, can it be considered
truly independent?

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:18.410
Independence is defined by a lack of dependence,
so the answer to that revolves around their

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.590
relationship with the US.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:27.090
The Federal Government’s official terminology
as to what tribal reservations are is “domestic

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.250
dependent nations.”

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:34.120
While that term originates from a 1831 Supreme
Court Case, it’s never used elsewhere to

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.449
define what any other geopolitical entity
is, either in the US or internationally, so

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:40.990
it’s rather ill-defined.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:47.400
Therefore, for all intents and purposes, “domestic
dependent nation” is essentially interchangeable

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.140
with the term “Indian Reservation.”

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:56.139
While what an American state is is well understood
and defined, what an Indian Reservation is

00:07:56.139 --> 00:08:01.510
is defined by a patchwork of decisions, guidelines,
legislation, and treaties.

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:06.610
For example, the Indian Citizenship Act of
1924 made it so that all remaining indigenous

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:11.980
people living in the US not already a citizen
would be granted such status, meaning, functionally,

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:17.600
that all those living on tribal nations are
obligated to pay taxes to the US Federal Government—as

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.710
the US, uniquely, taxes on citizenship and
not residency.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:26.819
However, when members of tribes live on tribal
land, they do not pay taxes to the states

00:08:26.819 --> 00:08:29.590
that that land is also a part of.

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.540
Window Rock, for example, is technically part
of both Arizona and the Navajo Nation, but

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:37.379
its residents only pay taxes to the latter.

00:08:37.379 --> 00:08:42.869
The tribes themselves, and therefore the reservations,
do not pay taxes to either state or federal

00:08:42.869 --> 00:08:47.309
governments—much like individual American
states themselves.

00:08:47.309 --> 00:08:52.369
Unlike states, however, tribal nations have
something similar to a citizenship system.

00:08:52.369 --> 00:08:57.160
Each tribe has the power to define who can
become members and what the process of enrollment

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.189
looks like.

00:08:58.189 --> 00:09:03.249
For example, in the case of the Navajo Nation,
members need to be at least 1/4th Diné, by

00:09:03.249 --> 00:09:06.800
lineage, and not a member of any other tribe.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.809
Building on that, private property is essentially
nonexistent on tribal nations—the land is

00:09:11.809 --> 00:09:17.149
held in trust by the US Federal Government,
and then managed by the tribal governments.

00:09:17.149 --> 00:09:22.360
Therefore, most residents either live in homes
rented out by the tribal housing authority,

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.160
or in mobile homes on public land.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:29.420
That's because banks won’t give loans to
build housing within reservations as the land

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:34.249
upon which they’d be built is publicly owned,
so it’d be impossible to repossess the homes

00:09:34.249 --> 00:09:36.059
if payments fell behind.

00:09:36.059 --> 00:09:41.389
They will loan for mobile homes, since they
can be repossessed more easily, but altogether,

00:09:41.389 --> 00:09:46.989
the policy of public land ownership effectively
means tribal nations have immigration control—you

00:09:46.989 --> 00:09:51.499
can only live in the Navajo Nation, for example,
if you qualify for government housing, and

00:09:51.499 --> 00:09:56.420
you only qualify for government housing if
you're either Diné or working in the nation.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:01.279
Adding to this, during the COVID pandemic,
the Navajo Nation enacted some of the strictest

00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:06.839
border restrictions in the United States—essentially
prohibiting access to all non-residents, except

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:11.829
when passing through on state highways—and
received no strong legal opposition to such

00:10:11.829 --> 00:10:12.829
action.

00:10:12.829 --> 00:10:18.290
So, altogether, the Navajo and other tribal
nations have a level of power over who can

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:23.600
live and visit their territory that eclipses
that of individual US states.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:28.540
However, there are other ways in which these
nations parallel states.

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:33.019
For example, the US Federal Government instructs
its agencies to treat any interaction with

00:10:33.019 --> 00:10:35.470
tribal nations as “government-to-government.”

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:41.540
Also, like states, tribal nations cannot engage
in formal relations with foreign governments,

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:46.829
enter into a state of war, or issue currency—all
key differentiators between nation-hood and

00:10:46.829 --> 00:10:47.829
country-hood.

00:10:47.829 --> 00:10:54.420
However, somewhat unlike states, the Navajo
Nation has this: a Washington office.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:59.249
This functions somewhat like the offices of
different state representatives in DC, however

00:10:59.249 --> 00:11:03.589
the Navajo Nation Washington Office is closer
to an embassy than these as it exclusively

00:11:03.589 --> 00:11:08.980
serves to represent the nation through non-voting
means to the US government and its agencies.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:13.529
The Navajo Nation wasn’t the first to attempt
to represent itself in Washington.

00:11:13.529 --> 00:11:19.649
In 1995, the Chickasaw Nation, of Oklahoma,
appointed Charles W. Blackwell as the first

00:11:19.649 --> 00:11:21.579
Tribal Ambassador to the US.

00:11:21.579 --> 00:11:26.959
In addition, they opened, what they called,
a Chickasaw Nation embassy in DC.

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:31.739
Now, neither the ambassador nor the embassy
had the diplomatic protections of those of

00:11:31.739 --> 00:11:36.490
foreign nations, so it’s unclear how legitimate
they were, but there were no challenges to

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:39.730
the nation setting up such a representative
system.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:44.600
The Cherokee Nation, also of Oklahoma, is
now going a step further, as they believe

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:48.399
they have a legal right to formal representation
in Washington.

00:11:48.399 --> 00:11:54.420
An 1835 treaty with the US gave the Cherokee
nation the power to send a non-voting representative

00:11:54.420 --> 00:12:00.100
to the US Congress—similar to those appointed
by the District of Columbia and US territories.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:05.600
Until 2019, the Cherokee never appointed such
a representative, but now decided to exercise

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:09.189
their rights when they appointed Kimberly
Teehee to fill the position.

00:12:09.189 --> 00:12:15.670
However, as of June 2021, she has yet to be
seated by Congress, meaning her power is not

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:16.790
yet formalized.

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:21.660
While it appears the nation has every legal
right to take such action, its a potentially

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.859
messy situation as the people of the Cherokee
tribe already have representation in Congress.

00:12:26.859 --> 00:12:32.490
While DC and the territories do not receive
voting congressional representation, the Cherokee

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:38.079
do as they live in Oklahoma, which, as a state,
has voting members in Congress.

00:12:38.079 --> 00:12:42.049
Having people represented twice in Congress
would be unprecedented.

00:12:42.049 --> 00:12:47.589
It’s unclear as of now whether Teehee will
be seated, but the reality is that this situation

00:12:47.589 --> 00:12:53.109
is like many involving tribal powers in the
US—they don’t know what they can do until

00:12:53.109 --> 00:12:54.660
they try.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.679
In fact, that was exactly what happened in
the 1970s when Russell and Helen Bryan, of

00:12:59.679 --> 00:13:04.829
the Minnesota Chippewa Tribe, received a property
tax bill for around $200 from a county government

00:13:04.829 --> 00:13:07.860
despite the fact they were living on tribal
land.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:11.329
Having never received such a bill before,
and not believing they were required to pay

00:13:11.329 --> 00:13:15.089
it, they brought it to a local lawyer, who
brought their case to the state courts.

00:13:15.089 --> 00:13:19.819
They lost that case, and they then lost their
appeal in the Minnesota Supreme Court, but

00:13:19.819 --> 00:13:23.489
then they brought it to the United States
Supreme Court.

00:13:23.489 --> 00:13:28.420
Their decision said that not only do states
lack the power to tax tribal members on their

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:32.429
reservations, but that states don’t have
the power to regulate any tribal activity

00:13:32.429 --> 00:13:34.160
on their nations.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:40.059
This was huge, and ideas soon arose as to
how to profit from this decision.

00:13:40.059 --> 00:13:45.540
Quite quickly, small-scale gambling operations
sprung up on reservations, and within years,

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.029
large tribal casinos started opening.

00:13:48.029 --> 00:13:53.999
Today, these casinos generate tens of billions
of dollars in revenue for tribal nations annually—especially

00:13:53.999 --> 00:13:57.529
for those lucky enough to be located near
major metro areas where gambling is illegal

00:13:57.529 --> 00:14:02.559
such as the Chippewa Nation, which operates
the largest Casino in America just 90 minutes

00:14:02.559 --> 00:14:03.860
from Dallas.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:08.709
Others, such as the Navajo Nation, are less
lucky.

00:14:08.709 --> 00:14:13.899
Located far from economic opportunity, their
unemployment rate is 49%, their average household

00:14:13.899 --> 00:14:21.339
income is $8,000, their poverty rate is 36%,
and one third of their households lack access

00:14:21.339 --> 00:14:23.359
to running water.

00:14:23.359 --> 00:14:29.079
Tribal reservations in the US exist in a sort
of in-between state—some sovereign powers

00:14:29.079 --> 00:14:33.339
are greater than those of states, but they
lack the true level of self-determination

00:14:33.339 --> 00:14:35.129
reserved for countries.

00:14:35.129 --> 00:14:39.680
However, being in such a lonely club, these
reservations don’t receive the attention

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:45.060
necessary to reform a system that, in many
cases, works against them.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:49.350
For example, the system of public ownership
of land means that it’s essentially impossible

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:53.149
for those living on reservations to own their
own home.

00:14:53.149 --> 00:14:57.359
That means they can’t build up wealth through
home ownership and pass it on to the next

00:14:57.359 --> 00:14:58.989
generation.

00:14:58.989 --> 00:15:03.389
Reservations are located in some of the most
rural areas of the US—in places already

00:15:03.389 --> 00:15:08.910
experiencing the effects of a deep rural-urban
economic divide—but being isolated from

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:13.480
the system of states, and ignored by the federal
government due to their political irrelevance,

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:18.859
they perpetually fall into the category of
“not my problem.”

00:15:18.859 --> 00:15:23.739
While there is opportunity in sovereignty,
it cannot be realized without trying, and

00:15:23.739 --> 00:15:29.040
trying new things is effectively impossible
for places so deeply entrenched in a cycle

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.100
of poverty.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:36.079
So, the answer to what the Navajo Nation or
any Native American reservation is is that

00:15:36.079 --> 00:15:37.279
nobody knows.

00:15:37.279 --> 00:15:42.489
They’re what the federal government lets
them be, and so far, there hasn't been enough

00:15:42.489 --> 00:15:47.470
time or money for them to explore their boundaries.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:54.949
